Stage 1 — Optimize N-Grams
	1.	Start with:
	•	minNGram=1
	•	maxNGram=1
	2.	Train & validate → baseline accuracy.
	3.	Increase maxNGram=2 (bigrams enabled).
	4.	Train & validate → compare accuracy.
	5.	Optionally test maxNGram=3 (trigrams).

Goal → Identify whether unigrams, bigrams, or trigrams work best.

Expected outcome:
	•	Usually, bigrams perform best for troubleshooting phrases like:
	•	“call failed”
	•	“wifi disconnected”
	•	“sim error”
	•	Trigrams sometimes help but may cause overfitting with <5K samples.

⸻

Stage 2 — Tune Sigma (Regularization)

Once we fix the best n-gram size:
	1.	Try sigma=0.5, sigma=1.0, and sigma=2.0.
	2.	Evaluate accuracy using k-fold cross-validation.

Key insights:
	•	Lower sigma → stronger regularization → better generalization on small datasets.
	•	Higher sigma → model fits training data more → risk of overfitting.

For <5000 samples, sigma ≈ 1.0 usually works best.

⸻

Stage 3 — Tune Feature Cutoff

Finally, tune featureCutoff to reduce noise:
	1.	Start with featureCutoff=1 → keep all features.
	2.	Try featureCutoff=2 and featureCutoff=3.
	3.	Compare results.

Recommendation:
	•	Use featureCutoff=1 if your vocabulary is small (e.g., <10K words).
	•	Use featureCutoff=2 if your dataset has noisy text like typos, emojis, or rare slang.

⸻

5. Recommended Cross-Validation Setup

To properly test, use 5-fold cross-validation:
	1.	Split your 5K samples into 80% train + 20% test.
	2.	Train the classifier for each configuration.
	3.	Measure:
	•	Accuracy → Correct classifications / total samples.
	•	F1-score → Better measure when some classes are smaller.
	•	Confusion matrix → Helps spot misclassified troubleshooting flows.

